1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:05,824
A coronary angiogram answers a question: is there a narrowing or

2
00:00:05,824 --> 00:00:10,197
blockage in an artery that supplies the heart muscle?

3
00:00:10,197 --> 00:00:13,598
In this example, the cardiologist reaches those

4
00:00:13,598 --> 00:00:16,513
arteries through the patient’s right wrist.

5
00:00:16,513 --> 00:00:21,858
We’ll follow the instruments to the heart, see how contrast creates

6
00:00:21,858 --> 00:00:26,230
the images, and return to the wrist for recovery.

7
00:00:28,730 --> 00:00:33,499
The heart muscle gets its blood supply from the coronary arteries.

8
00:00:33,499 --> 00:00:36,533
Plaque can build up in an artery

9
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:39,135
wall and make the channel narrower.

10
00:00:39,135 --> 00:00:42,169
An angiogram may be planned after a

11
00:00:42,169 --> 00:00:44,770
review of symptoms and other tests.

12
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:49,539
In some suspected heart attacks, the team may need an urgent

13
00:00:49,539 --> 00:00:53,441
angiogram and may go on to treat the artery.

14
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:01,720
For a planned angiogram, your team explains the test and reviews

15
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:06,938
your medicines, kidney health and any previous reaction to contrast.

16
00:01:06,938 --> 00:01:11,112
Follow their instructions about food, drink and medicines.

17
00:01:11,112 --> 00:01:14,243
In the cath lab, staff monitor

18
00:01:14,243 --> 00:01:17,373
your heart rhythm and blood pressure.

19
00:01:17,373 --> 00:01:19,461
You are usually awake.

20
00:01:19,461 --> 00:01:25,200
The team can give medicine to help you relax if needed.

21
00:01:27,730 --> 00:01:32,212
The radial artery runs on the thumb side of the wrist.

22
00:01:32,212 --> 00:01:35,065
Here the team checks the right wrist

23
00:01:35,065 --> 00:01:37,917
and uses ultrasound to see the artery.

24
00:01:37,917 --> 00:01:40,769
The skin is cleaned and the area

25
00:01:40,769 --> 00:01:43,214
is covered with a sterile drape.

26
00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:47,696
Local anaesthetic numbs the spot where the artery will be entered.

27
00:01:51,230 --> 00:01:54,253
Once the area is numb, a small

28
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:56,844
access needle enters the radial artery.

29
00:01:56,844 --> 00:01:59,866
Watch the tip cross the near wall

30
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:02,889
and come to rest inside the artery.

31
00:02:02,889 --> 00:02:07,207
That hollow needle gives the first wire a way in.

32
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:13,932
A short access wire passes through the

33
00:02:13,932 --> 00:02:17,134
hollow needle and into the radial artery.

34
00:02:17,134 --> 00:02:19,878
The wire points up the arm.

35
00:02:23,418 --> 00:02:26,950
The needle slides back out over the wire.

36
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:30,041
Watch how the wire stays in the

37
00:02:30,041 --> 00:02:32,691
artery and preserves the entry path.

38
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:41,173
The short radial sheath goes in over the access wire.

39
00:02:41,173 --> 00:02:44,633
A tapered insert, called a dilator, sits

40
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:48,093
inside the sheath as it goes in.

41
00:02:52,605 --> 00:02:57,137
The team removes the short access wire and the dilator.

42
00:02:57,137 --> 00:03:00,310
The outer sheath stays at the wrist.

43
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:04,843
It gives the next instruments a way into the artery.

44
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:13,223
A longer diagnostic guidewire passes through the sheath.

45
00:03:13,223 --> 00:03:17,482
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, lets the cardiologist

46
00:03:17,482 --> 00:03:21,133
see where the wire is going.

47
00:03:24,668 --> 00:03:28,907
The diagnostic catheter advances over the long guidewire.

48
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:34,206
The short sheath remains where the team entered the artery.

49
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:42,430
From the wrist, the wire and catheter follow arteries up the

50
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:45,420
arm, through the shoulder, and into the

51
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:48,411
large artery that leads to the aorta.

52
00:03:48,411 --> 00:03:53,110
Follow the tip as it comes around the arch and down

53
00:03:53,110 --> 00:03:56,101
the ascending aorta toward the aortic root.

54
00:03:56,101 --> 00:03:59,946
The coronary arteries begin just above the aortic valve.

55
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,335
At the aortic root, the long

56
00:04:08,335 --> 00:04:11,190
guidewire is withdrawn from the catheter.

57
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:16,423
Here the curved catheter tip is positioned at the left coronary

58
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:18,802
opening, above the aortic valve.

59
00:04:23,292 --> 00:04:27,490
The cardiologist injects X-ray contrast through the diagnostic catheter.

60
00:04:27,490 --> 00:04:32,621
For a moment, it outlines the channel that blood travels through.

61
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:37,752
Watch the left main artery fill and then see two of

62
00:04:37,752 --> 00:04:40,551
its main branches come into view.

63
00:04:40,551 --> 00:04:44,749
Now look at the same moment on the X-ray.

64
00:04:44,749 --> 00:04:49,413
The dark branching lines show where the contrast is flowing.

65
00:04:54,918 --> 00:04:58,184
In this wrist example, the same diagnostic

66
00:04:58,184 --> 00:05:01,451
catheter is shaped to reach both openings.

67
00:05:01,451 --> 00:05:06,584
It comes back into the aortic root, then the tip is

68
00:05:06,584 --> 00:05:09,385
repositioned at the right coronary opening.

69
00:05:12,918 --> 00:05:17,944
The cardiologist injects contrast separately at the right coronary opening.

70
00:05:17,944 --> 00:05:23,474
The right coronary artery and its branches briefly appear on X-ray.

71
00:05:23,474 --> 00:05:29,003
The imaging arm can move around the patient to record the

72
00:05:29,003 --> 00:05:31,014
arteries from different angles.

73
00:05:36,542 --> 00:05:39,427
On this example image, the contrast-filled

74
00:05:39,427 --> 00:05:42,311
channel is thinner at one spot.

75
00:05:42,311 --> 00:05:47,598
Another angle helps the cardiologist check where the narrowing is and

76
00:05:47,598 --> 00:05:49,521
how severe it looks.

77
00:05:49,521 --> 00:05:54,808
Sometimes a pressure measurement or imaging from inside the artery helps

78
00:05:54,808 --> 00:05:57,692
answer what the angiogram alone cannot.

79
00:05:57,692 --> 00:06:02,979
If no major blockage is visible and symptoms persist, the team

80
00:06:02,979 --> 00:06:05,383
may look for other causes.

81
00:06:09,918 --> 00:06:15,334
The next step depends on the findings, the symptoms, and whether

82
00:06:15,334 --> 00:06:17,303
the situation is urgent.

83
00:06:17,303 --> 00:06:20,750
It may be medicines, another test, angioplasty,

84
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:23,704
or a discussion about bypass surgery.

85
00:06:23,704 --> 00:06:27,151
Angioplasty uses a balloon and may place

86
00:06:27,151 --> 00:06:30,105
a stent to treat a narrowing.

87
00:06:30,105 --> 00:06:33,059
In selected cases the team can

88
00:06:33,059 --> 00:06:36,013
do that in the same session.

89
00:06:36,013 --> 00:06:38,968
Here, the test ends after imaging.

90
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:46,089
After the images are complete, the cardiologist

91
00:06:46,089 --> 00:06:49,698
withdraws the diagnostic catheter through the sheath.

92
00:06:49,698 --> 00:06:53,823
The sheath is still at the access site.

93
00:06:57,355 --> 00:07:00,480
Staff position a wrist compression band over

94
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:03,604
the puncture as the sheath comes out.

95
00:07:03,604 --> 00:07:08,068
The band puts pressure over the site to control bleeding.

96
00:07:11,605 --> 00:07:15,967
Staff check the puncture site and circulation in the hand.

97
00:07:15,967 --> 00:07:19,021
They gradually reduce the band pressure and

98
00:07:19,021 --> 00:07:21,638
remove it when it is safe.

99
00:07:25,168 --> 00:07:29,310
Staff check your pulse, blood pressure and wrist.

100
00:07:29,310 --> 00:07:32,935
They may check circulation in the hand.

101
00:07:32,935 --> 00:07:36,560
After an uncomplicated planned test, many patients

102
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:39,667
can go home the same day.

103
00:07:39,667 --> 00:07:41,738
Others need longer observation.

104
00:07:41,738 --> 00:07:47,434
Ask what the angiogram showed, how to care for the wrist,

105
00:07:47,434 --> 00:07:50,541
when to lift or drive again,

106
00:07:50,541 --> 00:07:53,648
and whom to contact with questions.

107
00:07:57,168 --> 00:08:03,309
Follow the written instructions for the dressing, activity, medicines and follow-up.

108
00:08:03,309 --> 00:08:07,776
A small bruise or mild soreness can happen.

109
00:08:07,776 --> 00:08:11,685
Contact the care team promptly for increasing

110
00:08:11,685 --> 00:08:15,035
redness or pain, drainage or fever.

111
00:08:18,542 --> 00:08:24,128
Call local emergency services for heavy bleeding or bleeding that continues

112
00:08:24,128 --> 00:08:29,714
despite firm pressure, rapidly growing swelling, a newly cold or numb

113
00:08:29,714 --> 00:08:34,284
hand, new chest pressure or sudden shortness of breath.

114
00:08:38,792 --> 00:08:43,291
The wrist gives the team a route to the heart.

115
00:08:43,291 --> 00:08:48,238
The catheter delivers contrast, the X-rays show the coronary arteries, and

116
00:08:48,238 --> 00:08:51,387
the images help guide the next decision.

117
00:08:51,387 --> 00:08:56,335
Before you go home, make sure you know your result and

118
00:08:56,335 --> 00:08:58,584
what to do during recovery.
